Read the POSIX monotonic clock directly: std::chrono::steady_clock has
1-SECOND resolution on this target, which pins the game to 1 fps.

libstdc++ for aarch64-phoenix is configured with none of its time backends --
_GLIBCXX_USE_CLOCK_MONOTONIC, _GLIBCXX_USE_CLOCK_REALTIME and
_GLIBCXX_USE_GETTIMEOFDAY are all #undef in c++config.h -- so
std::chrono::steady_clock::now() falls back to std::time() and advances in
whole seconds.

StkTime::getMonoTimeMs() is built on steady_clock, and MainLoop::getLimitedDt()
contains:

    while (dt == 0) { StkTime::sleep(1); ... dt = m_curr_time - m_prev_time; }

With a 1-second-granular clock dt is 0 for the whole second, so the loop sleeps
1 ms at a time until time() rolls over. The result is exactly one frame per
second, whatever the scene.

Measured on a Pi 4 before this patch, via a counter in the V3D winsys at the
page-flip: 32 frames per 32.00 s over six consecutive windows, i.e. 1000.0
ms/frame within 0.08%, of which only ~150 ms was GPU command-list submits, ~23
ms physics and ~0.15 ms the page-flip mailbox. The remaining ~825 ms was this
sleep. The invariance is the tell: identical figures at 1920x1080 and with the
whole deferred pipeline disabled, on two different tracks, and with sound off.

It also explains the in-game race clock independently: dt comes back as 1000 ms,
MainLoop caps it at MAX_ELAPSED_TIME (50 ms), so game time advances 0.05 s per
frame -- matching the 0.0497-0.0511 s/s measured off HDMI snapshots.

Guarded by __phoenix__ so other platforms keep steady_clock.

UPDATE 2026-09-09: the toolchain root cause is FIXED. phoenix-rtos-build
95d9fca adds gcc-16.2.0-12-libstdcxx-phoenix-features.patch, which hardcodes
the time backends (and ~20 other features) for *-phoenix*; a probe on hardware
measures steady_clock's tick at 2000 ns where it used to be 1000000000 ns. So
the description above no longer holds for a toolchain built after that commit,
and this patch is now a functional no-op -- clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC) is
exactly what steady_clock resolves to. It is kept rather than reverted because
it is correct either way and the port works; do not cite it as evidence that
libstdc++ is still broken. Full account:
docs/misc/2026-09-09-toolchain-silent-feature-audit.md in the coordination repo.

